      "claim": "Bayesian reasoning extends Boolean logic by handling uncertainty and probabilistic relationships instead of strict true/false values.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'Bayesian framework can generalize classical Boolean logic' [00:58:16\u201300:59:03]. The lecture explains that Boolean logic operates with true/false variables and modus ponens, while Bayesian reasoning uses probabilities to update beliefs with evidence."
    },
    {
      "claim": "In Boolean logic, if A implies B and B is true, then A cannot be determined (A could be true or false).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'From a Boolean logic point of view, we cannot say anything because we can derive two either from two or from false' [00:59:53\u201301:00:26]. This indicates that Boolean logic cannot determine A's truth value when B is true and A implies B."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The football example uses A as 'Werder Bremen wins the match' and B as 'football fans are celebrating'.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'So I know that if there are no wins, this is A, then the football fans will definitely be celebrating. So this is B' [01:00:27\u201301:00:57]. The context clarifies A and B as Werder winning and fans celebrating."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Bayesian reasoning calculates P(A|B) using the joint distribution P(A, B) = P(B|A) * P(A).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'And this joint distribution can be, for example, be written as PoB given A times PoA' [01:02:02\u201301:02:44]. This directly references the joint distribution formula."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior probability P(A|B) is higher than the prior probability P(A) when observing evidence B (fans celebrating).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'probably, well, probably clearly the posterior probability that worder has won the game will be larger than my prior probability when I observed people who are celebrating and waving the worder flex' [01:02:44\u201301:03:15]. This explicitly states the posterior probability update."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The prior probability P(A) for Werder winning is 0.1 (Werder is a weak team).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'So prior probability of A was, say, 1.0, 0.1. So very is not a good team. In comparison to the buyer.' [01:02:44\u201301:03:15]. This indicates a prior probability of 0.1 for Werder winning."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Bayesian reasoning allows updating beliefs with new evidence, which Boolean logic cannot do.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "The ASR states: 'This is how it works in theory' [01:03:16\u201301:03:44]. The context shows Bayesian reasoning updates probabilities with evidence, while Boolean logic cannot handle such uncertainty."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The phrase 'hyperability' in the ASR is a mishearing of 'Bayesian' (a speech error).",
